footer  (carry, after the build) [green] Built 2 parts, RangeBox_r2_Layout, RangeBox_r2_Carrier_Carrier and RangeBox_r2.SLDASM, reusing the 4 parts already built; no interference between the gears in any mode, where they stand. Its turning gears are mated to turn in each of its 3 modes, one configuration each (H, N and L), and SolidWorks reports no mate in error in any of them: it opens in H; to switch mode, double-click another mode's configuration in SolidWorks' ConfigurationManager. Drag Sun in SolidWorks and the rest of its train follow. RangeSlv slides along Output, to its place in each mode. The 3 copies of Planet turn on Carrier, and RangeBox_r2_Carrier_Carrier holds Carrier's axes and planes only: a plate and pins are yours to add. Ring stays fixed where it is placed: its shaft does not turn. A turn you give the gears in one mode can show in other modes, so a dog can stand across its sleeve in another; to put the gears back there, press Update SolidWorks with that mode showing. 1 thing to do by hand: Assign a material to each of the 2 parts; the build leaves it unset. Carried into RangeBox_r2, unsaved: your Sprocket-1, Sprocket-2 (was Sprocket-3), Sprocket-3 (was Sprocket-6) and 14 other components, each where it stood. SolidWorks would not describe UserFaceConc wholly (its reference could not be read ('method' object is not iterable)), so it is not made again. SolidWorks would not describe UserFaceCoin wholly (its reference could not be read ('method' object is not iterable)), so it is not made again. SolidWorks would not describe UserSunFace wholly (its reference could not be read ('method' object is not iterable)), so it is not made again. SolidWorks would not describe UserAsm6 wholly, so it is not made again. SolidWorks would not describe UserAxis9 wholly, so it is not made again. UserGearRoot was on a face of Sun, and Sun's part is new because its Flank changed, so it is not made again. UserGearFillet was on a face of Sun, and Sun's part is new because its Flank changed, so it is not made again. SolidWorks would not describe UserAxisLight wholly (its reference could not be read ('NoneType' object has no attribute 'FeatureByName')), so it is not made again. Your User Keyway Sketch and User Keyway stay in Gear_m1.25_N29, which Sun's new part replaces because its Flank changed (RangeBox.SLDASM and that file keep them). Your components were placed where they stood in H, the mode RangeBox.SLDASM showed; one no mate holds stands there in every mode. SolidWorks stopped the carry while setting your components' states in each mode: No component is kept as 'gear7', so whether it is free to turn cannot be read; that is a fault in Gear Generator, not in SolidWorks. It did not get to making the mates of Sprocket-1, Sprocket-3, Sprocket-4 and Chain-1 again, making the mates of Sprocket-2 again, making the mates of Housing-2 again, making the mates of Sprocket-5 again, making the mates of Sprocket-6 again, making the mates of Sprocket-7 again, making the mates of Sprocket-8 and Housing-1 again, making the mates of Sprocket-10 and Sprocket-11 again, making the mates of SprocketConf-1 again and checking every mode with your mates on. What was carried before it is in RangeBox_r2, unsaved, and RangeBox_r2 as built is saved. Assembly features, display states and appearances stay behind; sub-assemblies come over rigid, pattern instances as components of their own. If a part of yours was made in context of RangeBox.SLDASM, it still refers to that assembly's parts. SprocketLight-1 was lightweight in RangeBox.SLDASM and is resolved there now: SolidWorks loaded its part to carry it.
